  "description": "Bean qou-prjo, principle (i) from PR #4849: is a nucleon-symmetric readout FORCED by the corpus's treatment of an atom, and does the super-pairwise transverse residual survive it? Part A anatomises the corpus orbit identity tr_M(beta) = tr_M(rep): it is over S_Z x S_N only, and its Lemma 1 establishes it by showing such a permutation leaves the braid WORD unchanged -- the orbit of ordering strings is a SINGLE POINT for every atom while |S_Z x S_N| = Z!N! > 1. So symmetrization over the forced group is the IDENTITY MAP and R survives trivially, by derivation rather than by choosing a symmetrization. Part B is the decisive control: the corpus's OWN canonical scalar observable tr_M (Frobenius pairing sum_lambda y_lambda chi^lambda, certified by tr_M(1)=1, Markov M1 cyclic invariance and constant M2 stabilisation ratio) is itself CROSS-SPECIES ORDERING-DEPENDENT, so #4849's disqualifier -- a nuclear observable cannot be a function of the braid word -- applies verbatim to the canonical mass/binding trace and does not single out the transverse pairing. Part C reports the non-derived S_A operator average as a labelled counterfactual only; it selects nothing and does not cancel either. Blocker (i) is DISSOLVED, not solved; blocker (ii) (which germ observer pair + the representation gauge) is untouched and still binding. Nothing wired, no AME input, free_parameters = 0.",
